      <statement><p>Calculate the integral approximations <m>T_{6}</m> , <m>M_{6}</m> and <m>S_{12}</m> for</p>
<p><m>\int^{4}_{0} x^{6} \, dx</m>.</p>
<p><m>T_{6}</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>M_{6}</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>S_{12}</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>2566.03</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>2228.63</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <answer>
        <p>
          <m>2341.1</m>
        </p>
      </answer>
      <solution><m>f(x) = x^{6}</m>.  We divide [0, 4] into 6 subintervals of width



<m>\Delta x = \frac{4 - 0}{6} = 0.666667</m>



with endpoints <m>x_i</m> = 0, 0.666667, 1.33333, 2, 2.66667, 3.33333, 4, and midpoints <m>c_j</m> = 0.333333, 1, 1.66667, 2.33333, 3, 3.66667.  With this data, we get



<m>T_{6} = \frac{1}{2} \Delta x \left( f(x_0) + 2  f(x_1) + ... + 2  f(x_{N-1}) + f(x_N) \right) = \frac{1}{2} \cdotp 0.666667 \left( 0 + 0.175583 + 11.2373 + 128 + 719.188 + 2743.48 + 4096 \right) = 2566.03</m>



<m>M_{6} = \Delta x \left( f(c_1) + ... + f(c_N) \right) =  0.666667 \left( 0.00137174 + 1 + 21.4335 + 161.384 + 729 + 2430.12 \right) = 2228.63</m>



<m>S_{12} = \frac{2 M_{6}+T_{6}}{3}  = 2341.1</m>


</solution>
