      <solution><alert>(a)</alert>
<m>\int_{0}^{4}\,e^x\,dx = e^x\bigg|_{0}^{4} = e^4 - e^0 
  \approx 53.5982\ldots</m>




<alert>(b)</alert>
Computing the sums directly, since <m>\Delta x=2</m>, we have



LEFT(2) <m>= 2\cdot e^0 + 2\cdot e^{2} \approx 16.7782</m> 


RIGHT(2) <m>= 2\cdot e^{2} + 2\cdot e^{4} \approx 123.974</m> 


TRAP(2) <m>= \frac 1 2 ( 16.7782 + 123.974 ) \approx 70.3763</m> 


MID(2) <m>= 2\cdot e^{1} + 2\cdot e^{3\cdot 1} \approx 45.6077</m> 


and
SIMP(4) = (2 MID(2) + TRAP(2))/3 <m>\approx 53.8638</m>.



And the errors are just the actual value minus the approximate values,
or



error(LEFT(2)) = 36.8201; error(RIGHT(2)) = -70.3758;
error(TRAP(2)) = -16.7782; error(MID(2)) = 7.9906;
and error(SIMP(4)) = -0.2656.




<alert>(c)</alert>
Similarly, since <m>\Delta x = 0.5</m>, we have



LEFT(4) = 31.1929; error = 22.4053 


RIGHT(4) = 84.7911; error = -31.1928 


TRAP(4) = 57.992; error = -4.3937 


MID(4) = 51.4284; error = 2.1698 


SIMP(8) = 53.6162; error = -0.018.




<alert>(d)</alert>
For LEFT and RIGHT, we expect the error to go down by 1/2, and this
is very roughly what we see.  For MID and TRAP, we expect the error to
go down by 1/4, and this is approximately what we see.  For SIMP, we
expect the error to go down by 1/16, again as observed.



</solution>
